                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This game could have some potential, but I found it too grindy. The amount of effort to obtain a substantial amount of hormones could be toned down a lot. With the addition that the cost of suggestion goo conversion increases with each turn you are left trying to discover ways to push your goo multiplier up to 100 and at the same time have spent your society points to increase the entelechy count per round as well as the monads. But without the means to do so if all you have gotten are society points up front.. which is pretty easy to do if you aren't super careful in the forge.\n\nThere are a lot of secrets to unlock and a number of mechanics which seem to do nothing at all when first starting that .. yes, do things as you discover how they work. Discovering these is a definate pro for this game, but like above, I just wish the discovery aspect wasn't put behind a grind wall that resulted in the game taking days to complete !\n\nHere are some basics if you play this one to get you going. [spoiler] In the Osmos (screen with planets connected together) use WASD here to move around to different views and mouse scroll to zoom in/out. You can scroll all the way back to your cube, but you can also use esc to toggle cube/Osmos view. You can right click on a planet to move your focus, far as I can tell this actually does nothing other than changing the pivot point that WASD will act upon. A round in the Osmos proceeds through several turns until either you or the entelechy own all of the monads (planets). Tap space to advance 1 turn, ctrl I think is 10 turns. Each turn you gain resources which let you capture monads if you have enough, and you can redistribute your resources in a limited fasion using the resource buttons on the bottom of the screen. When you hover the mouse over a monad it shows the owner. Above the owner is a line. If the \tline is blue it means you currently have enough Osmos resources to attack, otherwise the line will be orange. If the line has a circle in the middle then it is a planet that you own. If the line is broken then it is not directly connected to a monad that you currently own and you cannot capture it yet. When you win you get a number of hormones that you can spend in the forge side of the cube. Working the forge correctly gains you the correct type of points, there is a little trick to it, I won't spoil everything. From there it's a matter of spending those points to unlock other parts, gain better mind injection options in the Osmos and enjoying a number of oddly abstract intermission videos as you progress through a set of rounds.[/spoiler]\n\nBarring the grindy nature, there are also a number of typos, where in a game that it counts to pay attention to the details, it turns out that the details have not exactly been paid attention to. This also goes for rendering in some places where help screens render without an exit button or the screen turns extra dark for some unknown reason making it super difficult to read. I could go on, but I'll stop here.\n\nCould be a good game, but needs polish.",
